# TextFSM template: Cisco IOS / IOS-XE `traceroute`.
#
# IOS emits three probes per hop on a single line (the default is
# `probe 3`); each probe is either `N msec` or `*` for a timeout.
# The destination header carries the target and optional source.
#
# Sample output:
#   Type escape sequence to abort.
#   Tracing the route to one.one.one.one (1.1.1.1)
#   VRF info: (vrf in name/id, vrf out name/id)
#     1 10.0.0.254 0 msec 0 msec 0 msec
#     2 192.0.2.1 [AS 64500] 1 msec 1 msec *
#     3 * * *
#     4 one.one.one.one (1.1.1.1) 8 msec 8 msec 8 msec
#
# IOS interleaves a `[AS N]` annotation between IP and the first
# `msec` figure when `traceroute mpls ... as-numbers` is enabled or
# when the BGP table is consulted. We capture that into asn1 / asn2
# / asn3 (treated as a per-hop AS, copied to every probe slot).
#
# A pure-star hop (no IP at all) lands on the second Start line.
# The numbered-probe convention (ip1/rtt_ms1, …) is consumed by the
# projector and expanded into three [pb.TracerouteProbe] entries.
# Note on the header capture: we deliberately do NOT capture the
# hostname into a per-record Value, because non-Filldown values in
# gotextfsm persist across line matches until the next Record. If
# the header captured `hostname1`, it would stick to every
# bare-IP hop row that followed (Cisco IOS does not print reverse-
# DNS names for transit hops by default). The header therefore
# uses a non-value group `(?:\S+\s+\()?` and leaves hostname1 to
# the hop lines.
Value Filldown target ([^)\s]+)
Value Filldown source (\S+)
Value Required ttl (\d+)
Value ip1 ([0-9a-fA-F:.]+)
Value hostname1 ([\w.\-]+)
Value asn1 (\d+)
Value rtt_ms1 (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)
Value rtt_ms2 (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)
Value rtt_ms3 (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)

Start
  ^Tracing\s+the\s+route\s+to\s+(?:\S+\s+\()?${target}\)?
  ^\s*${ttl}\s+${hostname1}\s+\(${ip1}\)(?:\s+\[AS\s+${asn1}\])?\s+${rtt_ms1}\s+msec\s+${rtt_ms2}\s+msec\s+${rtt_ms3}\s+msec -> Record
  ^\s*${ttl}\s+${ip1}(?:\s+\[AS\s+${asn1}\])?\s+${rtt_ms1}\s+msec\s+${rtt_ms2}\s+msec\s+${rtt_ms3}\s+msec -> Record
  ^\s*${ttl}\s+${ip1}(?:\s+\[AS\s+${asn1}\])?\s+${rtt_ms1}\s+msec\s+${rtt_ms2}\s+msec\s+\* -> Record
  ^\s*${ttl}\s+${ip1}(?:\s+\[AS\s+${asn1}\])?\s+${rtt_ms1}\s+msec\s+\*\s+\* -> Record
  ^\s*${ttl}\s+\*\s+\*\s+\* -> Record

